Negative control for forced-state.spec.ts (T3.12, AC8.4 — R6-10).

THE round-6 defect, restored in one line. The worker only decides what the tab's
NEXT request is answered with; it says nothing about the answers the tab is
already holding. Before this fix a preset armed a worker nobody then asked a
question of, so `force=empty` drew its chip and its ring over the LIVE rows, a
track's `replay` never put the recorded corpus on screen, and the whole feature
read as dead — driven live at qa-automation.localhost on 2026-08-12: exactly one
`GET /clients/:id/emails` for the entire session, no matter how many presets were
picked.

This mutant deletes the cache drop at the end of `reconcile` (and the import it
needs, so the mutated tree is exactly "the fix was never made"). Everything else
still passes: the worker registers, the handlers are right, the url carries the
preset and the chip renders. What must go red is the OBSERVABLE the operator was
denied — that a preset change makes the page ask again.

The assertion cannot be "a worker was started": that was already true while the
defect shipped. It has to read the revalidation itself — the query client being
told to drop what it holds, once per genuine preset change, on arming AND on
clearing.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: forced-state.spec.ts — the case proving an armed preset re-reads the page.

